      "content": "That distinction between the \"transient\" nature of first-generation cohorts and the potentially more resilient behavior of established communities is critical for our coefficient weighting.\n\nTo push the granularity of the model even further, we need to avoid over-generalizing. When we look at the \"high-velocity\" immigrant profiles, is there a statistically significant hierarchy in risk? For example, does the data suggest that certain groups—perhaps specific Latin American or Southeast Asian cohorts—exhibit higher rates of \"capital flight\" or faster commercial turnover compared to, say, West African or Caribbean communities? I'm looking to see if we can differentiate the weighting for these sub-groups to more accurately predict when a neighborhood is entering a period of rapid economic deterioration.",
